          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-6a12401e2ea08184\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Franco’s declaration is principally a considered avowal that he has loved Nikki for some time and now recognizes that love as real. Its timing and relational setting give it slight commitment-like force, but he does not explicitly undertake an obligation or shared future. His steady delivery and account of prior deliberation provide no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The declaration may help confirm or advance their relationship, creating limited P pressure, but its explicit force remains predominantly truth-conditional.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Franco directly tells Nikki, “I love you, Nikki,” during the wedding ceremony.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The surrounding wedding vows frame the exchange as relationally significant, while Franco’s explanation—he has loved Nikki for a while but waited to avoid rushing—presents the declaration as deliberate and sincere rather than reflexive. Nikki reciprocates, and the narration treats their exchange as confirmation of a real, steady love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you, Nikki,” he whispered, his voice steady and sincere.\",\"supports\":\"A direct, sincere avowal of Franco’s loving state strongly supports T; steady delivery alone does not support E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I’ve loved you for a while now. I just— I needed to make sure I didn’t rush this. But now... I know. I know it’s real.”\",\"supports\":\"His retrospective and deliberative explanation strongly supports T and weighs against interpreting the words as reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The declaration occurs while Franco and Nikki watch Calvin and Leila exchange wedding vows, after Nikki realizes that she has already found the love she wanted.\",\"supports\":\"The wedding setting gives the avowal some relational and commitment-associated significance, though it does not itself establish a substantial undertaking by Franco.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Nikki answers, “I love you too, Franco,” and the narration describes Franco as having patiently waited for her to find herself again.\",\"supports\":\"The reciprocal response confirms that the utterance is understood as a genuine romantic avowal within an established relationship.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The metadata identifies an affirmative present-tense explicit love construction and marks it KEEP with confidence 1.0.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted occurrence is an explicit first-person love declaration addressed to another person.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the declaration’s dominant avowal force, with slight P support for its relationship-confirming significance. Delivery and emotion remain contextual and do not require E, while no core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
